# RETARGETED TWICE: to 20261022170000 on 2026-10-22, then to 20261023080000 on
# 2026-10-23 when the audit writes required another `create or replace`. Each move is
# mechanical and each one is mandatory; the second was done in the same change as the
# redefinition, which is the rule the first occurrence produced.
#
# THE ORIGINAL FAILURE, AND THE REASON IT IS WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX.
#
# This mutation pointed at 20261021140400, where both functions were first defined. It
# applied cleanly there — `count: 1` matched, the edit landed — and the suite stayed GREEN,
# so the gate reported ESCAPED: a guard marked as controlled that is not controlled.
#
# Nothing was wrong with the guard. 20261022170000 redefines BOTH functions with
# `create or replace` to pin the scenario version, and migrations run in lexical order, so
# the mutated definition from 140400 was overwritten by the unmutated one from 170000
# before any test ran. The mutation was editing dead text.
#
# THE GENERAL HAZARD, for whoever redefines a function next: a `create or replace` in a
# later migration silently invalidates every mutation that targets the earlier definition.
# The mutation keeps applying, keeps reporting a match, and stops testing anything. The
# only reason this was caught is that the gate distinguishes ESCAPED from CAUGHT rather
# than grading on whether the suite went red — re-point the mutations in the same change
# that redefines the function.
#
# M18 — the owner predicate removed from BOTH idempotency lookups.
#
# THE LEAK THIS PREDICATE PREVENTS, MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS (port notes P1).
#
# Canon §4.6b stated, and verified at the time, that `app.require_user_id()` inside a
# SECURITY DEFINER function still resolves to the CALLER, so "a definer function is scoped
# to the caller's own rows BY THE SAME POLICY that scopes the app role". The first half
# still holds — `auth.uid()` reads a request-scoped GUC, not the session role. THE SECOND
# HALF DOES NOT. These functions were owned by `statesman_owner`, which had
# `rolbypassrls = false` and so was still subject to its own tables under FORCE (V8/V9);
# the policy ran inside the body. On Supabase they are owned by `postgres`, which IS
# BYPASSRLS, so RLS IS SKIPPED ENTIRELY INSIDE THESE BODIES.
#
# Most statements were already explicitly scoped. ONE WAS NOT: the idempotency lookup in
# both `submit_decision` and `append_revision` read `where dr.idempotency_token = p_token`
# with no owner predicate, because the policy supplied one. Under a bypassrls definer it
# matches ANY user's row, so user B replaying user A's idempotency token receives A's
# `revision_id` and `revision_no` with `replayed = true` — a cross-tenant identifier leak
# out of a code path whose entire purpose is to look as though it did nothing. It raises no
# error and affects no rows, so V7 covers it and nothing else would.
#
# Measured as a paired control (port notes §6, legs B2/B3):
#   * B2 — B replaying A's token against the FIXED code -> ST011, no row returned.
#   * B3 — the same probe against a mutant with the predicate removed -> B RECEIVED A'S
#          revision_id with replayed = true. The leak is real and the predicate is what
#          prevents it.
#
# BOTH LOOKUPS IN ONE MUTATION, because they are one obligation: a fix applied to
# `submit_decision` alone would leave `append_revision` leaking, and the two FIND blocks
# below are byte-identical, which is precisely why `count: 1` per block with distinct
# surrounding anchors is used rather than `count: 2` on the bare line.
#
# `dr_token_uq` is UNIQUE table-wide, so the predicate cannot change a legitimate replay by
# the token's own owner.
#
# THE CATCHER THIS ENTRY ORIGINALLY NAMED COULD NOT CATCH IT. It pointed at the SAME-USER
# replay test, and for a single user `and dr.user_id = me` is a no-op, so the mutation left
# that test green. A control that cannot fail for the thing it names is exactly what this
# gate exists to surface, and the gate surfaced it. The catcher below is a new
# cross-tenant test written for this predicate, whose assertion is the property directly:
# B never learns A's revision_id, by any path.
id: M18
title: `and dr.user_id = me` removed from the idempotency lookups in submit_decision and append_revision
expect: caught
proves: a SECURITY DEFINER body owned by a BYPASSRLS role gets no RLS for free, so every statement in it must be explicitly scoped (port notes P1)
proves: an idempotency replay cannot return another tenant's revision id
expect-test: a cross-tenant replay never returns the other user

file: supabase/migrations/20261023080000_audit_event.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
  select dr.id, dr.revision_no into rid, n
    from public.decision_revision dr
   where dr.idempotency_token = p_token and dr.user_id = me;
@@REPLACE
  select dr.id, dr.revision_no into rid, n
    from public.decision_revision dr
   where dr.idempotency_token = p_token;
@@END

file: supabase/migrations/20261023080000_audit_event.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
  select dr.id, dr.revision_no into rid, existing
    from public.decision_revision dr
   where dr.idempotency_token = p_token and dr.user_id = me;
@@REPLACE
  select dr.id, dr.revision_no into rid, existing
    from public.decision_revision dr
   where dr.idempotency_token = p_token;
@@END
